# shellcheck baseline for automation/
#
# Established by DRAFT-031. The point of this file is that the *bare*
# command exits 0 against unmodified working code:
#
#     shellcheck automation/*.sh          # picks this file up automatically
#
# Bare, because that is what `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md` and
# `docs/workflow-ci.md` all tell a developer and the CI tier (DRAFT-026a)
# to run. A gate that is red by default gets ignored, and one that only
# passes with flags nobody remembers is the same gate.
#
# Nothing here is suppressed without naming the issue that removes it.
# A suppression with no owner is not an exception, it is an untracked
# defect. When an owning issue lands, delete its line.

# --- What `automation/*.sh` now matches ---------------------------------
#
# **One file: `network-setup.sh`.** The shell/C launcher pipeline --
# `cleanup.sh`, `tcpdump.sh`, `run_analysis-<os>.sh`, `drakvuf-<os>.sh`,
# `preconfig-<os>.sh` -- was deleted on 2026-09-15, replaced by `sandboxd`.
# `network-setup.sh` survives because `sandboxd/daemon.py` executes it at
# every daemon start.
#
# So this baseline covers one script and the next one written, and the
# second of those is the reason it still says anything at all.

# --- Why `disable=` and not a severity floor ----------------------------
#
# The natural expression of this policy is `severity=warning`: every
# finding below is `info` or `style`, and `shellcheck -S warning
# automation/*.sh` does exit 0 today. It is not used because
# **`severity=` in this file is silently ignored by shellcheck 0.11.0** --
# measured, not assumed: an rc containing only `severity=warning` changes
# neither the output nor the exit status, while `disable=` in the same
# file works. Only the CLI `-S` flag applies a floor.
#
# So the choice was between a bare command that passes (this file) and a
# documented flag that everyone must remember (`-S warning`). This file
# wins, at a cost stated plainly below.
#
# Re-test `severity=` when shellcheck is upgraded. If it starts working it
# is strictly better than the blanket disable here, because it keeps the
# checks live for code written after this baseline.

# --- Dialect ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No `shell=` override, deliberately. Each file's own shebang is the
# authority, so a new per-OS script cannot be silently checked as a dialect
# it does not run under.
#
# `network-setup.sh` declares `#!/bin/sh` and is checked as POSIX sh, which
# is what actually interprets it: `/bin/sh` is `dash` on the Dom0 host, and
# `sandboxd` spawns it by path with no shell in between. Write POSIX --
# CLAUDE.md's `$0` versus `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}` rule is about this file.

# --- Accepted findings --------------------------------------------------
#
# SC1091  Not following: automation/platform.env was not specified as
#         input. Correct behaviour, not a defect: `network-setup.sh:367`
#         sources it from a path built at runtime from the script's own
#         location, so the file is not on shellcheck's input list. The
#         `# shellcheck source=` directive in the script records where it
#         lives.
#         Owner: none needed.
#         Verify with `shellcheck --norc --exclude=SC1091 automation/*.sh`,
#         which exits 0 -- this is the only finding in the tree.

# --- Retired suppressions -----------------------------------------------
#
# SC2006  RETIRED 2026-08-11 by DRAFT-028. Its only site in the repository
#         was network-setup.sh's `for i in \`seq 1 $1\``, replaced by a
#         POSIX while-loop.
#
# SC2086  RETIRED 2026-09-15. Unquoted expansions: 33 sites originally,
#         down to 9 after DRAFT-030 quoted every site carrying the sample
#         filename, and 0 now -- all nine lived in `cleanup.sh` and
#         `drakvuf-ubuntu.sh`, which no longer exist.
#
# SC2009  RETIRED 2026-09-15. `ps aux | grep` process discovery, 4 sites,
#         three of them in `cleanup.sh`. Its worst instance was the
#         `"tcpdump -i xenbr1.$VLAN"` match: unanchored, with `.` as a
#         regex wildcard, so VLAN 1 also matched xenbr1.10-19 once there
#         were more than 9 clones.
#
# **SC2086 and SC2009 were retired by DELETION, not by a fix**, and the
# distinction matters. DRAFT-011 owned both and never landed; the files
# carrying the findings went away instead. The defect itself was worth
# keeping, and it was: `sandboxd/proc.py::iter_processes` reads
# `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` and compares argv elements for equality precisely
# so the xenbr1.1-matches-xenbr1.10 class of bug cannot recur. See that
# module's docstring and `sandboxd/sweep.py::_kill_strays`.
#
# The upside is real: both checks are LIVE again for every file, so a new
# `preconfig-windows.sh` gets caught where it would previously have been
# waved through. Keep it that way -- if a new script needs one of these
# disabled, that is a finding to fix, not a line to restore here.
disable=SC1091